      "abstract": "or transcendental background of human existence. Dilthey\u2019s plan allowed for this background to be historically variable and context dependent. Relationship to Experimental Psychology and the Natural Sciences Conceptions of V\u00f6lkerpsychologie and descriptive psychology differed with respect to how they construed the relationship between psychology and the natural sciences. At one end of the spectrum, there was Dilthey\u2019s (in)famous 1894 dictum, according to which \u201cunderstanding\u201d or \u201cdescriptive psychology\u201d was an antidote to mistaken \u201cexplanatory\u201d and \u201cexperimental\u201d natural scientific psychology. Eduard Spranger followed Dilthey\u2019s lead, insisting that descriptive psychology is entirely independent from, and also superior to, psychology conceived as a natural science. At the other end of the spectrum, we find the conceptions of Lazarus, Steinthal, Wundt, Simmel, and the early Dilthey: for them, psychology was a natural science and, as such, the basis of allGeisteswissenschaften. All of these authors accepted that V\u00f6lkerpsychologie and descriptive psychology were different from experimental psychology. And yet they believed that their new psychological projects were complementary to experimental psychology and were in need of constant interaction with it. For example, Wundt developed his V\u00f6lkerpsychologie and his experimental work so that they went hand in hand. Both were needed in order to arrive at a theory of higher cognition. Moreover, Steinthal, Lazarus, and the early Dilthey were keen students of experimental-psychological work and did not regard experimental psychology as incompatible with descriptive-psychological or v\u00f6lkerpsychological forms of inquiry. Even Franz Brentano\u2019s \u201cdescriptive psychology\u201d was intended to mesh closely with the experimental approach. However, important shifts in the relationships between the different forms of psychology took place over time. The authors of early programmatic writings on V\u00f6lkerpsychologie and descriptive psychology wanted their new forms of inquiry to be continuous with early experimental psychology, and thus they were committed to a naturalistic outlook. Toward the end of the nineteenth century, however, visions for V\u00f6lkerpsychologie and descriptive psychology began to differ profoundly. Some representatives kept a commitment to the continuity between psychology and the natural sciences. For instance, this is true of Simmel, Wundt, Oswald K\u00fclpe, and Karl B\u00fchler, and even for Rudolf Carnap, who also appealed to a version of descriptive psychology in his early work. Other advocates of descriptive psychology rejected such a continuity and the naturalism it implied, seeking to demarcate descriptive psychology sharply from the natural sciences. The most radical proponents of this viewpoint were Eduard Spranger and Erich Rothacker. Among those who held on to naturalistic versions of",
